.TH RWYCTL 1 "" "Runway" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rwyctl \- read and change the boot slots of a Runway store
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rwyctl
[\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-\-io\-stats\fR]
[\fB\-\-store\fR \fIfile\fR]... [\fB\-\-device\fR \fIdev\fR]...
[\fB\-\-uuid\fR \fIuuid\fR] [\fB\-\-all\-devices\fR]
[\fB\-\-passphrase\-file\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-dek\-file\fR \fIfile\fR]
[\fB\-\-tpm\fR \fIdev\fR]
.I command
[\fIargument\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rwyctl
works on the boot slots of a Runway store, from the running system or from a build host.
A slot is a directory
.RI EFI/RUNWAY/CONFIG n /
that holds a
.B KERNEL
and, optionally, an
.BR INITRD ,
a
.BR DTB ,
the kernel command line
.BR ARGS
and a
.BR LABEL .
The slot with the highest revision that is not
.B FAILED
is started by
.BR RUNWAY.EFI .
.PP
Each slot has a state:
.BR INSTALLED " (new, never confirmed),"
.BR TESTING " (started once, not confirmed),"
.BR OK " (confirmed)"
and
.BR FAILED " (final)."
.PP
With the boot loop guard on
.RB ( LOOP\-GUARD ),
.B RUNWAY.EFI
counts how often it starts the same
.B OK
slot inside
.BR LOOP\-WINDOW .
The entry after
.B LOOP\-LIMIT
fails the slot (cause
.BR boot_loop )
and starts the next lower
.B OK
slot, else
.BR RECOVERY ,
with reason
.BR boot_loop .
A lone slot with nothing to fall back on is not failed.
.B confirm
resets the count.
.PP
A command that changes the store locks every member of it for its whole run, never waits for a lock,
and makes one commit.
It is all or nothing: on an error nothing is published.
.SS Finding the store
Without
.B \-\-store
or
.BR \-\-device ,
.B rwyctl
looks at the partitions of the running system, as
.B RUNWAY.EFI
does. It takes a partition of the store type (Extended Boot Loader) with a valid superblock.
A disk with no such partition is searched for a
.BR RUNWAY.DAT .
It never writes to a partition while it looks.
Name the store yourself with:
.TP
.BI \-\-store " file"
A store file, or
.IR image@offset [\fB+\fIlength\fR].
Repeat it to name every member of a mirror.
.TP
.BI \-\-device " dev"
A partition such as
.I /dev/sda2
or
.BI PARTUUID= uuid\fR.
May be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-\-uuid " uuid"
Use the store with this UUID when there are several.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-devices
Use every store that is found.
.PP
Several stores and none chosen is an error (exit 3).
A mirror is the same store (same UUID) on up to 8 disks.
A write reaches every member that is current.
A member that is missing or behind is not written, and
.B rwyctl
warns.
.SS Mounted store
When
.BR rwy\-fuse (1)
holds the store,
.B rwyctl
connects to the daemon's RPC socket, passes its arguments and open file descriptors over
.BR SCM_RIGHTS ,
and
.B rwy\-fuse
executes the command in-process against its mounted store without closing the store or dropping its locks.
When no daemon answers on a locked store (for example another offline
.B rwyctl
holds the lock), the command fails with exit 5 at once.
.PP
.BR init ,
.B check \-\-repair
and
.B reencrypt
are refused (exit 5): unmount the store first.
.SS Keys
An encrypted store needs a key.
.B rwyctl
tries, in this order: the option
.B \-\-dek\-file
(32 raw bytes) or
.BR \-\-passphrase\-file ,
the variables
.B RWY_DEK_FILE
and
.BR RWY_KEY ,
a TPM 2.0 unseal when the store has a TPM key slot, and then a prompt on the terminal.
Without a terminal it exits with 4 and does not wait.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v
Say why a partition was skipped while looking for a store, and when a command runs via
.B rwy\-fuse
RPC on a mounted store.
.TP
.B \-\-io\-stats
Run the command, then print on the standard error the calls made to the store and the system calls made to the files.
.TP
.BI \-\-passphrase\-file " file"
Read the passphrase from a file.
.TP
.BI \-\-dek\-file " file"
Read the data key from a file.
.TP
.BI \-\-tpm " dev"
TPM device or Unix socket (default
.IR /dev/tpmrm0 ).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Look at the store
.TP
.B stores
List the stores that are found: UUID, number of members, generation, whether it is encrypted, and the
member files.
.TP
.B members
Print the path of each member of the store (the boot store, else the only one, or the one of
.BR \-\-uuid ),
one per line, to be used by a mount helper. It fails (exit 3) when there is no store or several and none
is chosen, and when a
.B RUNWAY.DAT
is on a file system that is not mounted.
.TP
.B status \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
One line per slot (revision, state, tries, watchdog, cause of a failure, label, BLS entry name,
metadata), then the global values
.RB ( BOOTED ", " REASON ", " WATCHDOG ", " SLOTS ", " BOOT\-DELAY ", " QUOTA ", " LOOP\-GUARD ", " LOOP\-LIMIT ", " LOOP\-WINDOW ", " USED ", " LAST\-EVICT ,
.BR RECOVER_NEXT ", " COUNTER ", " RECOVERY ", " RECORD ", " ANCHOR ", " INCOMPLETE ).
After them, one
.B MIRROR\-MEMBER
line for each mirror member that has a health counter: its UUID, its state now
.RB ( online ", " lagging " or " absent ),
the counters
.RB ( LAGGING ", " SYNC_FAILED ", " WRITE_FAILED ", " MISSING )
and the last event with the record sequence it happened at.
.B RECORD\-RESETS
tells how many times the state record was found damaged.
A healthy store prints none of these lines.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
print the status as a JSON object.
Its keys are the lower case names of the lines above, with
.B \-
written as
.BR _ ,
except that
.B slots
is the array of slots and
.B SLOTS
is
.BR slots_limit .
A value that is not set is
.BR null .
The
.B mirror
array and
.B record_resets
are always present.
.TP
.BR get " [\fINAME\fR] \fIFIELD\fR"
Print one value and nothing else.
Exits with 1 when the value is not set.
.TP
.BR slots " [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-label\fR \fILABEL\fR]"
Print the slot names, one per line, in slot order. With
.BR \-\-label ,
print only the slots whose
.B LABEL
is exactly
.IR LABEL ;
nothing is printed, and the exit status is 0, when none matches.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
print the matching slot names as a JSON array.
It only reads the store, so it works on a mounted store too.
.TP
.B check
Check
.I EFI/RUNWAY/
and exit with 8 on a violation.
.B check \-\-repair \-\-unlock
repairs the revision counter and the state record offline; it needs
.B \-\-store
or
.BR \-\-device .
Orphan blobs (content blobs that no slot links) and staging files are warnings;
.B \-\-repair
removes them.
.B check \-\-blobs
also hashes every content blob and reports one whose content is not its name
(it reads all the blobs).
With
.B \-\-repair
a slot that links such a blob is marked
.B FAILED
with
.B CAUSE
.BR bad_blob ;
the blob is never renamed or rewritten.
.TP
.B flight show \fR[\fB\-\-raw\fR]
Show the flight recorder of the boot events.
It is plaintext and needs no key.
.TP
.B log show \fR[\fB\-\-raw\fR]
Show the update journal (install, update, confirm, fail, remove).
.TP
.B log verify
Check the hash chain of the update journal.
.PP
With
.B \-\-raw
the output is
.IR key = value
lines.
.SS Change the slots
.TP
.B init
Create an empty
.IR EFI/RUNWAY/ .
.B install
refuses without it.
.TP
.B install \-\-kernel \fISRC\fR [\fIoptions\fR]
Write a whole new slot, state
.BR INSTALLED ,
in one commit.
Print the slot name and its revision.
A payload file keeps its modification time (64-bit seconds).
A payload that is a copy keeps its change time too;
a link to a content blob (--dedup) has the change time of the link.
The options are
.BI \-\-initrd " SRC" ,
.BI \-\-dtb " SRC" ,
.B \-\-dedup
(share identical payloads via content blobs in
.IR EFI/RUNWAY/BLOBS/ ),
.BI \-\-args " TEXT" | @FILE,
.BI \-\-label " TEXT" | @FILE,
.BI \-\-entry " NAME" | @FILE,
.BI \-\-meta " TEXT" | @FILE,
.BI \-\-tries " N"
(boots an unconfirmed slot gets, default 1),
.BI \-\-watchdog " S"
and
.BI \-\-name " CONFIGn" .
Without
.B \-\-name
the slot is
.BI CONFIG n
with n one above the highest.
.I SRC
is a file, or
.BI blob: HASH
for a content blob (64 lower case hex digits) that is already in
.IR EFI/RUNWAY/BLOBS/ .
Without
.B \-\-dedup ,
the slot receives its own copy of each payload with independent physical blocks, isolating candidate slots and fallback slots from shared block damage.
When
.B \-\-dedup
is given, a file is stored once as
.IR EFI/RUNWAY/BLOBS/HASH ,
the SHA-256 of its content, and the slot links it.
.IR RECOVERY/
always keeps its own copy of the data.
When
.B QUOTA
is set and the new slot would pass it, the older slots that the plan names
(incomplete, then
.BR FAILED ,
then the oldest
.BR OK )
are removed first, in a commit of their own, and the new slot follows in the next commit.
Each removal is an
.B EVICT
record in the update journal.
The booted slot, the newest
.B OK
slot (and the next one, without
.BR RECOVERY/ )
and a slot under test are never removed.
When they and the new slot do not fit, nothing is removed and the exit code is 7.
On an enrolled store the anchor is checked first (exit 6 when it cannot be trusted).
When the removals leave enough free blocks but no run long enough, the store is defragmented before the install.
The sources are checked before anything is removed.
.TP
.B update \-\-kernel \fISRC\fR [\fIoptions\fR]
Like
.BR install ,
without naming a slot, and it keeps the number of slots at
.BR SLOTS .
Below
.B SLOTS
it adds a slot.
At
.B SLOTS
it replaces one in the same commit: a
.B FAILED
slot first, then the oldest
.B OK
slot.
It never replaces the booted slot, the newest
.B OK
slot or a slot under test (exit 6 when none may go).
When
.B QUOTA
is set,
.B update
needs no
.B SLOTS
and works as described for
.B install
above.
.TP
.BR confirm " [\fINAME\fR]"
Mark a slot
.BR OK .
The default is the booted slot.
It is refused (exit 6) for a
.B FAILED
slot, and when
.B RUNWAY.EFI
could not record its choice and started another slot.
Confirming the booted slot also deletes the
.B RunwayBootGuard
EFI variable, which counts the boots for the boot loop guard.
Confirming a slot that is already
.B OK
only does that, with no write to the store.
If the variable cannot be deleted (for example a read\-only
.IR efivarfs ),
the slot stays confirmed, a warning is printed and the exit status is 1.
.TP
.BR fail " [\fINAME\fR]"
Mark a slot
.BR FAILED .
This is final.
.TP
.B set \fR[\fINAME\fR] \fIFIELD VALUE\fR
Set a global value:
.B WATCHDOG
(seconds),
.B SLOTS
(1 to 64),
.B BOOT\-DELAY
(0 to 30 seconds),
.B QUOTA
(10 to 95 percent of the usable store),
.B LOOP\-GUARD
(\fBon\fR or \fBoff\fR),
.B LOOP\-LIMIT
(2 to 255 entries) or
.B LOOP\-WINDOW
(30 to 86400 seconds).
.TP
.B remove \fINAME\fR
Delete a slot.
.TP
.B recover\-next \fR[\fB\-\-cancel\fR]
Boot
.B RECOVERY
once at the next boot, or cancel the request.
.TP
.B put \fIPATH SRC\fR, \fBcat\fR \fIPATH\fR
Write a file into the store, or print one.
.SS Recovery and watchdog
.TP
.B recovery area set \-\-kernel \fISRC\fR [\fB\-\-initrd\fR \fISRC\fR] [\fB\-\-dtb\fR \fISRC\fR] [\fB\-\-args\fR \fITEXT\fR|@\fIFILE\fR]
Write the plaintext recovery area of an encrypted store.
It needs no key.
It writes every member, and the copy that is not in use, so a power cut keeps the old bundle.
Needs
.B \-\-store
or
.BR \-\-device .
.TP
.B recovery area show
Show the copies.
Exits with 8 when a member has no valid bundle or the members differ.
.TP
.B recovery set \-\-unlock \-\-kernel \fISRC\fR [\fIoptions\fR]
Install the
.B RECOVERY
slot. It keeps its own copy of the data, also for a
.BI blob: HASH .
.TP
.B wdt set \-\-unlock \fISRC\fR...
Put the EFI watchdog drivers in
.IR EFI/RUNWAY/WDT/ .
Each file is stored as a content blob and linked under its base name.
.B RUNWAY.EFI
tries them one by one until one arms.
.SS Loader area
.TP
.B loader set \fIFILE\fR [\fB\-\-wdt\fR \fIDIR\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-tries\fR \fIN\fR]
Stage a new loader binary on trial in the plaintext loader area.
Writes the inactive copy across every mirror member, computes and verifies SHA\-256 hashes, writes the bundle header last, and updates the state record to mark the copy as a pending trial.
Optionally includes watchdog driver applications from
.I DIR
and sets trial timeout (default 300 seconds) or tries count (default 3).
.TP
.B loader show \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
Display both bundle copies, their validity status, sequence counters, entry hashes, and the state record.
Exits with 8 when a member has no valid bundle, an invalid state record, or when mirror members hold differing copies.
.TP
.B loader commit
Promote the pending trial copy to confirmed good immediately without requiring a reboot.
.TP
.B mirror clear
Drop the health counters of every member and write the record, after a repair.
It needs
.B \-\-store
or
.BR \-\-device .
.SS Encrypted stores
.TP
.B anchor status
.TQ
.B anchor enroll \fR[\fB\-\-pcr7\fR \fIHEX\fR]
.TQ
.B anchor reset \fR[\fB\-\-pcr7\fR \fIHEX\fR]
The anti-rollback counter in the TPM.
.B enroll
defines the counter for the store (policy on PCR 7), raises it once and records its value.
.B reset
does the same after it removed a counter that is there, for a restored copy, a changed PCR 7 or a new TPM.
Both need the store key and the TPM.
.B \-\-pcr7
binds the policy to the given PCR 7 value (64 hex digits), for a device that is prepared offline.
On an enrolled store,
.BR install ,
.BR update ,
.B remove
and
.B fail
are refused (exit 6) while
.B ANCHOR
is not
.BR ok .
.TP
.B reencrypt \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Change the data key and the key slot.
The options are
.BR \-\-old\-key ,
.BR \-\-new\-key ,
.BR \-\-old\-passphrase ,
.BR \-\-new\-passphrase ,
.BR \-\-old\-passphrase\-file ,
.BR \-\-new\-passphrase\-file ,
.BR \-\-old\-dek\-file ,
.B \-\-new\-dek\-file
and
.BI \-\-new\-dek\-out " file"
(write the new data key to a private file).
The store needs free space for a second copy of the live data, and the command refuses before it writes
(exit 7) if there is not enough.
After a power cut the store can be mounted with one of the two keys, and the command can be run again.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done.
.TP
.B 1
I/O error, or a value that is not set.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
No store found, or several and none chosen.
.TP
.B 4
The store is locked: no usable key.
.TP
.B 5
The store is busy: another command or
.BR rwy\-fuse (1)
holds it.
.TP
.B 6
Refused by the layout rules.
.TP
.B 7
No space.
.TP
.B 8
.B check
or
.B log verify
found violations.
.PP
Messages go to the standard error and start with
.BR rwyctl: .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
Seconds since 1970 to use as the time of every change, for a reproducible image.
The modification time of a copied file is capped by it, and its change time is set to it.
Without it, files get the wall-clock time.
.BR mkstore (1)
honours it too.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Directory for the scratch copy of an input that is on a mounted store (default
.IR /var/tmp ).
.TP
.B RWY_KEY
Passphrase of an encrypted store.
.TP
.B RWY_DEK_FILE
File with the 32 raw bytes of the data key.
.TP
.B RWY_TPM
TPM device or Unix socket, if
.B \-\-tpm
is not given.
.TP
.B RWYCTL_ROOT
Prefix for
.IR /proc ,
.I /sys
and
.IR /dev ,
for tests with fake trees.
.TP
.B RWY_RUN_DIR
The directory of the socket of
.BR rwy\-fuse
(default
.IR /run/rwy\-fuse ).
.SH EXAMPLES
Install an image, then confirm it once the system has started well:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rwyctl install \-\-kernel vmlinuz \-\-initrd initrd.img \-\-args @cmdline
rwyctl confirm
.fi
.RE
.PP
Work on a store file with a passphrase:
.PP
.RS
rwyctl \-\-store store.img \-\-passphrase\-file pw status
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fsck.runway (8),
.BR rwy\-fuse (1),
.BR mount.runway (8)
